# NAME

@PACKAGE_NAME@ — Manipulation of MIME body parts

# SYNOPSIS

**package require Tcl 8.6-**\
**package require @PACKAGE_NAME@ ?@PACKAGE_VERSION@?**

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::initialize** ?**-canonical** *type/subtype* ?**-param** {*key value*}...? ?**-encoding** *value*? ?**-header** {*key value*}...?? (**-file** *name* | **-string** *value* | **-parts** {*token1* ... *tokenN*})\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::finalize** *token* ?**-subordinates all** | **dynamic** | **none**?\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::getproperty** *token* ?*property* | **-names**?\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::getheader** *token* ?*key* | **-names**?\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::setheader** *token* *key value* ?**-mode write** | **append** | **delete**?\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::getbody** *token* ?**-decode**? ?**-command** *callback* ?**-blocksize** *octets*??\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::copymessage** *token* *channel*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::buildmessage** *token*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::parseaddress** *string*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::parsedatetime** (*string* | **-now**) *property*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::mapencoding** *encoding_name*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::reversemapencoding** *charset_type*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::parseparams** *value*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::formatparams** *value* *params*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::word_encode** *encoding* *method* *string* ?**-charset_encoded** *boolean*? ?**-maxlength** *length*?\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::word_decode** *encoded-word*\
**@PACKAGE_NAME@::field_decode** *field*

# DESCRIPTION

The **@PACKAGE_NAME@** package provides the commands to create and
manipulate MIME body parts. It is a fork of the **mime** package from
tcllib (version 1.7.2), made self-contained (it depends on nothing outside
the Tcl core), with many bugs and RFC compliance problems fixed. Where
fixing them meant changing behaviour, the fix was preferred over
compatibility: see **DIFFERENCES FROM TCLLIB MIME** below, and the
**porting-mime-to-myme** skill distributed with the package.

## Characters and octets

Messages are octets (bytes, i.e. strings with no characters above
U+00FF), and the body text and header values they contain are
characters:

- A **-string** given to **-canonical text/\*** is characters. It is
  encoded in the part's **charset** parameter when the part is created
  (raising an error if the charset can't represent them). If there is no
  charset parameter and the text isn't ASCII, **charset=utf-8** is added.
- Any other **-string** (another **-canonical** type, or a message to
  parse) must be octets, and a **-file** is read as octets, whatever the
  type.
- **getbody** returns the body's octets, or with **-decode**, characters
  (decoded from the charset).
- **buildmessage** returns octets, and **copymessage** writes octets
  (whatever the channel's **-encoding**).
- Header values are characters. On output, non-ASCII values of the
  unstructured fields **Subject**, **Comments** and
  **Content-Description** are RFC 2047 encoded, and other fields are
  written as UTF-8 (RFC 6532: use **word_encode** for display names in
  address fields where that isn't supported). Parsed header fields are
  decoded from UTF-8 where valid (otherwise octet for character), and are
  not RFC 2047 decoded: use **field_decode** for that.
- **Content-Type** parameter values (and those handled by
  **parseparams** / **formatparams**) are characters: RFC 2231
  continuations and charsets are decoded on parse and used as needed on
  output.

## Headers

Header fields are unfolded when parsed (RFC 5322 section 2.2.3), and
folded on output at whitespace (outside quoted-strings) into lines of at
most 78 characters where possible. A value that contains a line break, or
that would need a line of more than 998 octets, is an error.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::initialize** ?**-canonical** *type/subtype* ?**-param** {*key value*}...? ?**-encoding** *value*? ?**-header** {*key value*}...?? (**-file** *name* | **-string** *value* | **-parts** {*token1* ... *tokenN*})
:   Creates a MIME part and returns a token representing it.

    - If the **-canonical** option is present, then the body is in
      canonical (raw) form and is found by consulting either the **-file**,
      **-string**, or **-parts** option.

      In addition, both the **-param** and **-header** options may occur
      zero or more times to specify **Content-Type** parameters (e.g.,
      **charset**) and header keyword/values (e.g.,
      **Content-Disposition**), respectively.

      The *type/subtype* may be followed by parameters (as in a
      Content-Type header field); **-param** takes precedence over them.

      Also, **-encoding**, if present, specifies the
      **Content-Transfer-Encoding** when copying the body. If it is
      absent, an encoding is chosen based on the content: none for ASCII
      text with short lines, **quoted-printable** for text that is mostly
      ASCII (or has long lines), and **base64** otherwise. Only **7bit**,
      **8bit** or **binary** are allowed for **multipart/\*** and
      **message/\*** parts.

      A **-string** for **text/\*** is characters, any other is octets
      (see **Characters and octets** above).

    - If the **-canonical** option is not present, then the MIME part
      contained in either the **-file** or the **-string** option (as
      octets) is parsed, dynamically generating subordinates as
      appropriate. Parts of **multipart/digest** default to
      **message/rfc822**. Only **message/rfc822**, **message/global** and
      **message/news** are parsed as containing a message; other
      **message/\*** types (such as **message/partial** and
      **message/delivery-status**) are leaves. A multipart body without a
      close-delimiter ends at the end of the input, and its last part is
      taken as **application/octet-stream**.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::finalize** *token* ?**-subordinates all** | **dynamic** | **none**?
:   Destroys the MIME part represented by *token*. It returns an empty
    string.

    If the **-subordinates** option is present, it specifies which
    subordinates should also be destroyed. The default value is
    **dynamic**, destroying all subordinates which were created by
    **@PACKAGE_NAME@::initialize** together with the containing body part.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::getproperty** *token* ?*property* | **-names**?
:   Returns a string or a list of strings containing the properties of a
    MIME part. If the command is invoked with the name of a specific
    property, then the corresponding value is returned; instead, if
    **-names** is specified, a list of all properties is returned;
    otherwise, a dictionary of properties and values is returned.

    The possible properties are:

    **content**
    :   The type/subtype describing the content

    **encoding**
    :   The "Content-Transfer-Encoding"

    **params**
    :   A dictionary of "Content-Type" parameters, RFC 2231 decoded

    **parts**
    :   A list of tokens for the part's subordinates. This property is
        present only if the MIME part has subordinates.

    **size**
    :   The approximate size of the content (unencoded)

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::getheader** *token* ?*key* | **-names**?
:   Returns the header of a MIME part as a dictionary with
    possibly-redundant keys.

    If *key* is provided, then a list of values of matching names, without
    regard to case, is returned.

    If **-names** is provided, a list of all keys is returned.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::setheader** *token* *key value* ?**-mode write** | **append** | **delete**?
:   Sets, appends to, or deletes the header *key*. Returns a list of
    strings containing the previous value associated with the key.

    The value for **-mode** is one of:

    **write**
    :   The *key*/*value* is either created or overwritten (the default).

    **append**
    :   A new *value* is appended for the *key* (creating it as necessary).

    **delete**
    :   All values associated with the key are removed (the *value*
        parameter is ignored).

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::getbody** *token* ?**-decode**? ?**-command** *callback* ?**-blocksize** *octets*??
:   Returns a string containing the body of the leaf MIME part represented
    by *token* in canonical form.

    If the **-command** option is present, then it is repeatedly invoked
    with a fragment of the body as this:

        uplevel #0 $callback [list "data" $fragment]

    (The **-blocksize** option, if present, specifies the maximum size of
    each fragment passed to the callback.)

    When the end of the body is reached, the callback is invoked as:

        uplevel #0 $callback "end"

    Alternatively, if an error occurs, the callback is invoked as:

        uplevel #0 $callback [list "error" reason]

    Regardless, the return value of the final invocation of the callback
    is propagated upwards by **@PACKAGE_NAME@::getbody**.

    If the **-command** option is absent, then the return value of
    **@PACKAGE_NAME@::getbody** is a string containing the MIME part's
    entire body.

    Without **-decode** the body is returned as octets (in the part's
    charset). With **-decode** it is decoded from the charset (US-ASCII if
    none) into characters.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::copymessage** *token* *channel*
:   Writes the MIME part represented by *token* to *channel* as octets
    (the channel's **-encoding** is set to iso8859-1 while doing so, and
    restored afterwards), and returns an empty string.

    Line endings are written as **\\n**, so the channel's **-translation**
    determines the line endings of the result: configure it as **crlf**
    to produce wire format (as for SMTP). CRLF line endings already
    present in the content are normalized first, so they are not doubled.
    The content of parts with a **Content-Transfer-Encoding** of
    **binary** is written without end-of-line translation.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::buildmessage** *token*
:   Returns the MIME part represented by *token* in wire format: as
    octets, with CRLF line endings. Line endings in the content are
    handled as for **@PACKAGE_NAME@::copymessage**.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::parseaddress** *string*
:   Takes a string containing one or more 822-style address specifications
    and returns a list of dictionaries, one element for each address
    specified in the argument. If the string contains more than one
    address they will be separated by commas.

    Each dictionary contains the properties below. Note that one or more
    of these properties may be empty.

    **address**
    :   local@domain (or just local, if there is no domain)

    **comment**
    :   822-style comment

    **domain**
    :   the domain part (rhs)

    **error**
    :   non-empty on a parse error

    **group**
    :   this address begins a group

    **friendly**
    :   user-friendly rendering: the phrase (unquoted, and RFC 2047
        decoded), or failing that the comment or local part

    **local**
    :   the local part (lhs)

    **memberP**
    :   this address belongs to a group

    **phrase**
    :   the phrase part

    **proper**
    :   822-style address specification

    **route**
    :   822-style route specification (obsolete)

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::parsedatetime** (*string* | **-now**) *property*
:   Takes a string containing an RFC 5322 date-time specification and
    returns the specified property. The fields are those of the date-time
    as given, in its own zone (in the local zone for **-now**). Comments
    are ignored, and a missing, military or other unknown zone is taken
    as -0000 (RFC 5322 section 4.3).

    The list of properties and their ranges are:

    **hour**
    :   0 .. 23

    **lmonth**
    :   January, February, ..., December

    **lweekday**
    :   Sunday, Monday, ... Saturday

    **mday**
    :   1 .. 31

    **min**
    :   0 .. 59

    **mon**
    :   1 .. 12

    **month**
    :   Jan, Feb, ..., Dec

    **proper**
    :   822-style date-time specification

    **rclock**
    :   elapsed seconds between then and now

    **sec**
    :   0 .. 59

    **wday**
    :   0 .. 6 (Sun .. Sat)

    **weekday**
    :   Sun, Mon, ..., Sat

    **yday**
    :   1 .. 366

    **year**
    :   1900 ...

    **clock**
    :   the time, in seconds since the epoch

    **zone**
    :   minutes east of UTC

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::mapencoding** *encoding_name*
:   Maps Tcl encodings onto the proper names for their MIME charset type.
    This is only done for encodings whose charset types were known. The
    remaining encodings return "".

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::reversemapencoding** *charset_type*
:   Maps MIME charset types onto Tcl encoding names. Those that are
    unknown return "".

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::parseparams** *value*
:   Parses a header field value consisting of a token and parameters,
    such as Content-Disposition (RFC 2183), and returns a list of the
    token (lower case) and a dictionary of the parameters, RFC 2231
    decoded.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::formatparams** *value* *params*
:   The inverse of **parseparams**: returns a header field value made of
    the token *value* and the parameters in the dictionary *params*, using
    RFC 2231 for values that are long or not ASCII. For example, for an
    attachment:

        -header [list Content-Disposition \
            [@PACKAGE_NAME@::formatparams attachment [dict create filename $name]]]

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::word_encode** *encoding* *method* *string* ?**-charset_encoded** *boolean*? ?**-maxlength** *length*?
:   Returns *string* as RFC 2047 encoded-words, separated by "\\n " (a
    fold), each at most *length* (default 66) characters. *encoding* is a
    Tcl encoding name (e.g. **utf-8**), and *method* is **base64** or
    **quoted-printable**. *string* is characters, unless
    **-charset_encoded** is true, in which case it is already encoded in
    *encoding*.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::word_decode** *encoded-word*
:   Decodes a single encoded-word, returning a list of the Tcl encoding,
    the method and the decoded octets.

**@PACKAGE_NAME@::field_decode** *field*
:   Returns *field* with its encoded-words decoded into characters.
    Whitespace between adjacent encoded-words is dropped (RFC 2047 section
    6.2), and encoded-words that can't be decoded (e.g. an unknown
    charset) are left as they are.

# DIFFERENCES FROM TCLLIB MIME

The **porting-mime-to-myme** skill, distributed with the package in
**claude/skills/**, describes these in detail for porting code.

- The package is **@PACKAGE_NAME@** and its commands are in the
  **::@PACKAGE_NAME@** namespace, so it can be loaded alongside tcllib's
  **mime**.

- No dependencies outside the Tcl core: tcllib's **base64**, **md5** and
  **tcl::chan::memchan** are no longer needed, and **Trf** is no longer
  used when present (so the behaviour doesn't change depending on whether
  it happens to be installed). Requires Tcl 8.6 or later.

- **copymessage** and **buildmessage** emit a single
  **Content-Transfer-Encoding** header (mime 1.7.2 emitted it twice for
  canonical leaf parts, which strict MTAs such as AWS SES reject).

- **copymessage** and **buildmessage** no longer double CRLF line endings
  in content (tcllib ticket 67ace2b948), and leave the content of
  **binary** parts untranslated.

- Large **-file** bodies encoded as **base64** are emitted as proper 76
  character lines (mime 1.7.2 produced over-length lines at each 32766
  byte read boundary without Trf).

- Multipart boundaries are generated without md5.

- Parsing no longer silently degrades to text/plain in an interpreter
  where **::errorCode** doesn't exist yet (tcllib ticket 0534941436).

- **field_decode** no longer drops encoded spaces at the end of an
  encoded-word (tcllib ticket d3f66f9a75).

- Choosing a **Content-Transfer-Encoding** when **-encoding** isn't given:
  CRLF line endings are no longer mistaken for non-ASCII content or
  trailing whitespace (tcllib ticket f44bcbf068), and non-ASCII text is
  never sent without one (which would declare it 7bit).

- Quoted-printable text encodes CRLF line endings as line breaks rather
  than **=0D**.

- Bodies, messages and headers use the characters/octets model described
  in **Characters and octets** (mime encoded **-string** inconsistently:
  as characters in the system encoding for 7bit/8bit with
  **buildmessage**, and as octets for base64 and quoted-printable).
  **word_encode** takes characters by default (**-charset_encoded 0**).

- Header fields are unfolded when parsed and folded on output; values
  containing line breaks are rejected; non-ASCII Subject, Comments and
  Content-Description are RFC 2047 encoded on output; parsed header
  fields are decoded from UTF-8. Content-Type is emitted on one (folded)
  line.

- RFC 2231 parameters are decoded on parse (**params** has the plain
  names and decoded values) and generated for long or non-ASCII values;
  **-canonical** accepts inline parameters.

- Only message/rfc822, message/global and message/news are parsed as
  embedded messages; a multipart **-string** without a close-delimiter
  parses (like **-file**) instead of raising an error.

- **parsedatetime** fields are in the date-time's own zone (not the local
  zone), **proper** keeps the zone given, and missing zones are -0000.

- **parseaddress** no longer adds **@**[**info hostname**] to a bare local
  part, and **friendly** is unquoted and RFC 2047 decoded.

- **field_decode** leaves encoded-words it can't decode as they are,
  rather than raising an error.

- Generated multipart boundaries have the form **=_myme_**... (no space).

- RFC compliance fixes: **parsedatetime** handles numeric zones (the
  **zone** property failed for all of them), comments, offsets over 12
  hours and unknown zones (as -0000); quoted-printable decoding handles
  CRLF soft line breaks, and encoding protects lone "." and "From " lines
  correctly; Q encoded-words encode phrase specials such as "," and "(";
  multipart delimiters may have transport padding; parameter values are
  escaped on output and quoted-pairs undone on input, and RFC 2231
  extended values (**name\*=**) are not quoted; parts of a
  **multipart/digest** default to **message/rfc822**; encoded-words may
  have a language suffix (**=?utf-8\*en?Q?...?=**);
  **Content-Transfer-Encoding** may contain comments; and **-encoding**
  base64 or quoted-printable is rejected for **multipart/\*** and
  **message/\*** parts.

- Re-serializing a parsed part copies its **Content-Transfer-Encoding**
  as-is: one is no longer invented for parts that didn't have one (which
  corrupted them), and unrecognised ones (e.g. x-uuencode) no longer
  raise an error.

# EXAMPLE

```tcl
package require @PACKAGE_NAME@

# Body text is characters: encoded as UTF-8 (charset=utf-8 is added)
set body [@PACKAGE_NAME@::initialize -canonical text/plain \
    -string "Caf\u00e9 \u2615\n"]

# Other bodies are octets
set h [open report.pdf rb]
set pdf [read $h]
close $h
set att [@PACKAGE_NAME@::initialize -canonical application/pdf \
    -encoding base64 -string $pdf \
    -header [list Content-Disposition [@PACKAGE_NAME@::formatparams attachment \
        [dict create filename "R\u00e9sum\u00e9.pdf"]]]]

set msg [@PACKAGE_NAME@::initialize -canonical multipart/mixed -parts [list $body $att]]
@PACKAGE_NAME@::setheader $msg From    "Sender <sender@example.com>"
@PACKAGE_NAME@::setheader $msg To      "<recipient@example.com>"
@PACKAGE_NAME@::setheader $msg Subject "Caf\u00e9 report"   ;# RFC 2047 encoded
set wire [@PACKAGE_NAME@::buildmessage $msg]   ;# octets, CRLF line endings
@PACKAGE_NAME@::finalize $msg -subordinates all
```

